# Levenberg–Marquardt

Levenberg–Marquardt (LM) minimizes nonlinear least-squares objectives with
scaled damping. It regularizes the Gauss–Newton model and rejects unsuccessful
trials, making it a useful starting choice for smooth curve-fitting problems.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/levenberg_marquardt/struct.LevenbergMarquardt.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                   |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth sum of squared residuals, or an adapted robust loss  |
  | Evaluations | `Residual` and `Jacobian`                                     |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                                |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained; use bounded LM or TRF for box bounds           |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, plus nalgebra-sparse and faer-sparse |

## When to use it

Consider LM when residuals and a Jacobian are available but an undamped
[Gauss–Newton](../gauss-newton/) step is unreliable. Its diagonal scaling
accounts for differences in Jacobian column magnitudes. For ill-conditioned
dense Jacobians, compare [LM with pivoted QR](../levenberg-marquardt-qr/), which
avoids forming `JᵀJ`.

[Marquardt (1963)](#ref-marquardt-1963) describes scaled damping, and [Moré
(1978)](#ref-more-1978) develops the implementation and scaling approach. LM
remains a local optimizer; damping does not remove dependence on the start.

## How it works

The Cholesky variant solves `(JᵀJ + μD) h = -Jᵀr`, where `D` tracks the running
maximum of the Gram diagonal. Initially zero columns receive a unit floor. A
positive ratio of actual to predicted reduction accepts a trial. Rejected trials
reuse the current linear model.

The default `LmDamping::TrustRegion` chooses damping to fit a scaled radius
measured by `sqrt(hᵀDh)`. An undamped step is used when it fits. Radius
initialization and updates follow MINPACK's `lmder`, while a bracketed search
replaces `lmpar`'s parameter selection. Basin retains its own acceptance and
stopping rules, so this is not a complete MINPACK port.

`LmDamping::Nielsen` instead updates damping directly from the gain ratio,
following [Nielsen (1999)](#ref-nielsen-1999) and [Madsen, Nielsen, and Tingleff
(2004)](#ref-madsen-nielsen-tingleff-2004), §3.2.

## Example

This affine fitting problem isolates the residual and Jacobian interface. The
solver sets an optimality tolerance, and the executor limits iterations.

<!-- compile: solver-levenberg-marquardt -->
```rust
use basin::{DenseMatrix, Executor, Jacobian, LevenbergMarquardt, Residual};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Fit;

impl Residual for Fit {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = Vec<f64>;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn residual(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Output, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![x[0] - 1.0, 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0)])
    }
}

impl Jacobian for Fit {
    type Jacobian = DenseMatrix;

    fn jacobian(&self, _: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Jacobian, Self::Error> {
        Ok(DenseMatrix::from_row_slice(2, 2, &[1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 2.0]))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver =
        LevenbergMarquardt::new().with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
    let result = Executor::from_start(Fit, solver, vec![0.0, 0.0])
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.param()[1] - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-12);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Configure damping separately from convergence

Trust-region damping is the default for both scalar types.
`with_initial_step_bound` sets its initial radius factor (default `100`). To use
Nielsen damping, select `with_damping(LmDamping::Nielsen)` and tune `with_tau`
(default `1e-3`); `tau` does not affect trust-region damping.
`with_max_inner_attempts` limits model retries, with default `50`.

### Interpret the stopping test

Only `with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` is enabled by default, testing
`‖Jᵀr‖∞ ≤ 1e-8`. Choose its threshold for residual and parameter units. Optional
orthogonality, model-reduction, trial-step, and scaled-radius tests combine with
OR. The radius test applies only to trust-region damping. Small steps or model
reductions can occur under heavy damping while the gradient remains large,
including with robust losses.

Use gradient tests alone when stationarity is required, or guard native relative
progress tests with `with_absolute_progress_gradient_tolerance`. This guard does
not apply to executor stops or observed progress checks. Keep execution budgets
on the executor and inspect the report: an unchanged rejected finite trial can
produce `NumericalNoProgress`, and exhausted retries or numerical breakdowns
produce `SolverFailed`. Neither certifies accuracy.

## Implementation

Levenberg–Marquardt publishes matching parameters and cost through `PointState`.
It computes the gradient internally; the state does not expose it through
`GradientState`. Residuals, Jacobians, and model caches belong to the solver.
Fresh runs reset them, while exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain them.

For ordinary residuals, the reported cost is `0.5 * sum(r[i]^2)`. The solver
requires `Residual` and `Jacobian`, without a `CostFunction` implementation.
Wrapping the problem in
[`RobustLeastSquares`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/least_squares/struct.RobustLeastSquares.html)
selects a robust loss and residual scale. The adapter supplies a safeguarded
model, while costs and convergence use the robust objective and its gradient.
Custom Jacobians additionally need `ScaleRowsInPlace` for robust losses.

Supported dense pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The default `Vec` path needs
no BLAS/LAPACK provider.

Nalgebra-sparse and faer-sparse Jacobians are also supported; their Gram
matrices must include the diagonal in the CSC pattern for damping. Use
`LevenbergMarquardt::default()` for `f32`.

